---
Attack summary against both parts of the claim (worked in an isolated cp -a
copy at /tmp/claude-1000/.../scratchpad/x2check, toolchain go1.26.6).

(a) TestRevision_DoesNotBumpOnCacheMissLoad genuinely reaches the
decode-migration re-save path.
- Read decodeSettings/normalizeLoadedWhatIfSettings (settings.go:279-339):
  the seeded legacy JSON has no "persons" key, so len(settings.Persons)==0
  is true and the migration branch (changed=true, Persons synthesized from
  legacy current_age) is unconditionally taken — not a vacuous seed.
- The test's own new assertion (`len(settings.Persons)==0` -> Fatal) directly
  guards against the vacuous-seed counter-scenario the task asked me to
  construct.
- Mutation test: changed line 618's `sm.saveInternal(settings)` to
  `sm.saveInternalAndBump(settings)` inside the `changed` branch of
  loadInternalContext. Result: TestRevision_DoesNotBumpOnCacheMissLoad FAILED
  ("a cache-miss load bumped the revision: 1 -> 2"), confirming the test is a
  real, non-tautological guard on saveInternal (no bump) vs
  saveInternalAndBump (bump) — the two are otherwise textually
  interchangeable so a weaker test would not have caught this. Reverted.

(b) All 24 mutators return prepare.Clone(settings), cache never escapes.
- Grepped every `func (sm *SettingsManager) ... (*models.WhatIfSettings, ...)`
  in settings.go: exactly 24 methods that load, mutate, save, and return
  settings (AddIncomeSource..PurgeRemovedOneTimeExpense family, UpdateSettings,
  ApplyOverrides, UpdateSettingsWithPersons, UpdateSpendingPhases, the
  healthcare/big-ticket/one-time-expense family, CreateScenario). `git diff
  HEAD -- settings.go` shows the mechanical conversion applied to all 24
  return sites uniformly in one diff (`return settings, nil` ->
  `return prepare.Clone(settings)`, or explicit `cloned, err :=
  prepare.Clone(...)` for the 3 multi-return methods) — confirmed by direct
  diff read, not just grep.
- Error paths audited line-by-line for all 24 (awk+grep over
  settings.go:806-1907): every non-success return is `nil, err` (or
  `nil, "", 0, err` / `nil, 0, err`); none leak the raw, partially-mutated
  `settings` object on a validation/conflict/not-found error.
- Mutation test: reverted AddIncomeSource's return to raw `settings, nil`.
  `go test ./internal/services/retirement/...` failed with
  TestMutatorReturnDoesNotAliasCache: "AddIncomeSource returned the cached
  object, not a copy" — confirms this guard is real and would catch a
  regression on the one mutator it covers. Reverted.
- Real gap found: TestMutatorReturnDoesNotAliasCache exercises ONLY
  AddIncomeSource ("stands in for the whole family" per its own comment) — it
  is not a per-mutator loop, so the other 23 conversions are NOT covered by an
  automated aliasing regression test, only by the uniform diff and my manual
  code-path audit above. This is an honest, disclosed test-coverage
  limitation, not a hidden defect: the diff itself (git diff HEAD) shows the
  fix applied identically at all 24 sites, so the claim as stated ("24
  mutators... return prepare.Clone") is true today; a future edit to any of
  the other 23 mutators could silently reintroduce the bug without a test
  failing. Reporting for the record; not fatal to this verdict since claim (b)
  is a statement about the current code, which I independently verified.
- Nested-pointer/shallow-copy concern: read prepare.Clone/DeepCopy
  (prepare/prepare.go). DeepCopy is a JSON marshal/unmarshal round trip
  (independent structs throughout); Clone additionally deep-copies the
  json:"-" fields (CurrentAge, SpouseAge, RothConversion.PerYearOverrides map
  — copied element-by-element into a fresh map, not aliased). Cross-checked
  against TestCloneCarriesEveryJSONOmittedField (prepare/clone_test.go, a
  pre-existing test outside this manifest, unmodified by X2, currently
  passing) which reflects over the whole WhatIfSettings type graph and
  explicitly asserts non-aliasing for every json:"-" field found by walking
  the struct, including map/slice-kind checks. Found one other json:"-" field
  in the codebase, TaxOptimizerCandidate.BracketFillFeedback
  (models/whatif.go:1618) — confirmed NOT reachable from WhatIfSettings (it's
  on an unrelated analysis-result struct), so it is correctly out of scope for
  Clone/the reflection walk. No shallow-copy hole found.
- Multi-return Clone-error-after-save branch (UpdateSettings, ApplyOverrides,
  UpdateSettingsWithPersons): if prepare.Clone errors after
  saveInternalAndBump already succeeded (and bumped sm.revision), all three
  return `nil, ..., err` — a "phantom failure" where the write landed but the
  caller is told it failed. Read all production callers
  (handlers/whatif/handlers_live.go, handlers/whatif/handlers_rates.go,
  services/mcpsvc/plan/register.go): every one checks `err != nil` and
  returns/aborts before touching the returned settings pointer, so this
  cannot nil-deref a caller. Because Clone's DeepCopy is the same
  json.Marshal that saveInternal already ran successfully on the identical
  object moments earlier, this path is practically unreachable (would need
  Marshal to succeed once and fail on the same data immediately after) —
  noting for the record, not treating as a live defect.
- Concurrency: `go test -race -count=1 ./internal/services/retirement/`
  (69.5s) — PASS, no races, including existing goroutine-based tests that
  exercise CreateScenario/ApplyOverrides against concurrent readers.
- Cross-package fallout: `go test -count=1 ./internal/services/mcpsvc/plan/
  ./internal/handlers/whatif/` — PASS.
- Baseline: `go build ./...` clean; `go vet ./internal/services/retirement/...`
  clean; `gofmt -l` clean on all 3 manifest files; full `go test -count=1
  ./...` — all packages PASS including internal/services/dataloader (no
  sibling-caused casualty observed in this copy).

No FAIL-worthy defect found after mutation testing of both halves of the
claim. Recommend the boss note the 1/24 automated-coverage gap on
TestMutatorReturnDoesNotAliasCache as a follow-up item (extend it to loop
over the full mutator family, or accept the diff-uniformity argument as
sufficient) — not blocking.
